{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 10, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and a leader of the County of Tripoli a Crusader state in the Holy Land. He was born around 1140 and died in 1187. Raymond was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. After his father's death in 1152 Raymond II was succeeded by his son Raymond III who was still a minor at that time. \n## *Early Life and Regency*\nRaymond's early life was marked by his regency in the Kingdom of Jerusalem. In 1164 he was taken captive by Nur ad-Din Zangi during the Battle of Harim and was released in 1173. After his release Raymond became regent for King Amalric I's son Baldwin IV who was suffering from leprosy. \n## *Regency in the Kingdom of Jerusalem*\nAs regent Raymond played a crucial role in the governance of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a member of the Haute Cour and advised the king on various matters. Raymond's regency was marked by his efforts to secure the kingdom's borders and to negotiate with the Muslim states surrounding the kingdom. \n## *Conflict with Guy of Lusignan and Downfall*\nRaymond's later years were marked by conflict with Guy of Lusignan the husband of Sibylla of Jerusalem. The conflict ultimately led to the Battle of Hattin in 1187 where the Crusader forces were defeated by Saladin. Raymond died soon after the battle. \nRaymond's death marked the end of an era in the County of Tripoli and the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was succeeded by his godson Raymond IV Count of Tripoli who was the son of Bohemond III of Antioch. Raymond III was known for his military prowess and his diplomatic skills. He played a crucial role in the history of the Crusader states and his legacy continued to shape the politics of the region for centuries to come. Throughout his life Raymond was involved in various military campaigns and diplomatic efforts. He was a key figure in the Crusa